      115  '''Lima'''{{efn|{{IPAc-en|ˈ|l|iː|m|ə|audio=LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-Lima.wav}} {{respell|LEE|mə}}; {{IPA|es|ˈlima|lang|audio=ES-Lima.ogg}}), founded in 1535 as the '''Ciudad de los Reyes''' ({{IPA|es|sjuˈða(ð) ðe los ˈreʝes}}, Spanish for "City of [[Biblical Magi|Kings]]"}} is the [[List of metropoli
      210  [[File:La Independencia del Perú.jpg|thumb|[[Proclamación de la Independencia del Perú|Painting]] of [[José de San Martín]] during the Declaration of Independence of Peru in the [[Plaza Mayor, Lima|Plaza Mayor]], on July 28, 1821]]
      211  A combined expedition of Argentine and Chilean independence fighters led by General Don [[José de San Martín]] landed in southern Lima in 1820, but did not attack the city. Faced with a naval blockade and guerrilla action on the mainland, Viceroy [[José de la Serna, 1st Count of the Andes|José de la
      212  Once General [[José de San Martín|San Martín]] proclaimed the [[Peruvian War of Independence|independence of Peru]], {{cite news|url=https://www.congreso.gob.pe/historia/|title=Reseña Histórica del Congreso|website=Congreso de la República del Perú |last1=Pe |first1=vis. com }} Lima became the capit
      213  ===Republican era===
      214  [[File:Defunct Main City Gate of Lima, 19th century.jpg|thumb|Gate of the [[Walls of Lima|colonial wall of Lima]], the "[[Gate of Lima|Arco del Puente]]" its partially wooden structures were consumed by a fire caused by street vendors in 1879. {{cite web |date=3 December 2021 |title=Un arco para Lim
      219  [[File:Plaza Mayor Lima 19XXs.png|left|thumb|The [[Metropolitan Cathedral of Lima]] in [[Plaza Mayor, Lima|Plaza Mayor]] in 1910.]]
      220  At the beginning of the 20th century, the construction of avenues that would serve as a matrix for the development of the city began. {{cite book|title=Dignos de su arte: sujeto y lazo social en el Perú de las primeras décadas del siglo XX|author=Kathya Araujo|year=2009|publisher=Iberoamericana Edit
      221  In the 1930s the great constructions began with the remodeling of the [[Government Palace, Peru|Government Palace of Peru]] and the [[Palacio Municipal de Lima|Palacio Municipal]]. These constructions reached their peak in the 1950s, during the government of [[Manuel A. Odría]], when the great build
      222  Also in those years a phenomenon began that changed the configuration of the city, which was the massive immigration of inhabitants from the interior of the country, producing the exponential growth of the capital's population and the consequent urban expansion. {{cite web|url=https://www4.congreso.
      223  [[File:Plaza San Martín 1970.jpg|thumb|[[Plaza San Martín, Lima|Plaza San Martín]] in 1970]]
      224  As an emblematic point of this expansion, in 1973 the self-managed community of Villa El Salvador (current district of [[Villa El Salvador]]) was created, located 30 km south of the city center and currently integrated into the [[Lima metropolitan area|metropolitan area]]. {{cite web|url=http://www.
      225  === 21st century ===
      226  [[File:Miraflores - Lima, Peru at Night.jpg|alt=|thumb|View of the Bajada Balta in the [[Miraflores District, Lima|Miraflores District]] at night]]
      232  [[File:Lima, Peru - 51315703245.jpg|thumb|Lima as seen from the [[International Space Station]]]]
      233  The urban area covers about {{convert|800|km2|sqmi|abbr=off}}. It is located on mostly flat terrain in the [[Sechura Desert|Peruvian coastal plain]], within the valleys of the [[Chillón River|Chillón]], [[Rímac River|Rímac]] and [[Lurín River|Lurín]] rivers. The city slopes gently from the shores of
      234  [[Lima metropolitan area|Metropolitan Lima]] covers {{convert|2672.28|km2|sqmi}}, of which {{convert|825.88|km2|sqmi|abbr=on}} (31%) comprise the actual city and {{convert|1846.40|km2|sqmi|abbr=on}} (69%) the city outskirts. {{Cite web |url=https://www.inei.gob.pe/media/MenuRecursivo/publicaciones_d
